{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-160866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $W$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in W.$\n- The set $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)=(0)^{2}+1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple not in $W.$ For example, let $p(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $p(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $p(0)=1.$ However, \n\n$$\n2\\cdot p(0)=2\\cdot ({0}^{2}+1)=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n Hence, $k\\cdot p(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $h(x)\\in U,$ where $h(x)=3x.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $U$ contains only polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $h(x)=3x$ is *not* a polynomial of this form. So, $h(x)\\notin U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x)=a{x}^{3}\\in U,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient, and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\nk\\cdot p(x)=k(a{x}^{3})=(ka){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $(ka)$ is a real coefficient, we conclude that $(ka){x}^{3}\\in U,$ i.e., $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $g(x)\\in C,$ where $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- For every $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n- The set is closed under addition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials whose degree is less than or equal to $2,$ however\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡({x}^{3})=3>2.\n$$\n\nSo, $g(x)={x}^{3}\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ then $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 2$ and $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(q(x))\\le 2.$ Since $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)$ is less than or equal to the maximum of the degrees of $p$ and $q$, we deduce that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-160874" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160874" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160874" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $K$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=-1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in K,$ where $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in K$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in K.$\n- The set is closed under addition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}\\in K$ because \n\n$$\np(0)=(0-1)^{3}=(-1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $K$ such that their sum is *not* in $K.$ For example, let $f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $K$ since $f(0)=g(0)=-1.$ However,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=-1+(0-1)^{3}=-2\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin K.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $W$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={2}^{x}.$\n- For any $f(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in W.$\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={2}^{x}\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)={2}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple *not* in $W.$ For example, let $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $f(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $f(0)=1.$ However,\n\n$$\n2\\cdot f(0)=2\\cdot {2}^{0}=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nHence, $k\\cdot f(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2},$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)-q(x)=q(x)-p(x).$\n- There are polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U$ such that $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)\\ne p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as \n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{3}+0\\cdot {x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \nHence, $0\\in U.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)={x}^{3}\\in U$ and the zero-polynomial $q(x)=0\\in U.$ Then \n\n$$\np(x)-q(x)=p(x)-0={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\nq(x)-p(x)=0-p(x)=-{x}^{3}\\ne {x}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have \n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)],\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-162392" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162392" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162392" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-74700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- For any $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U,$ we have $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)=p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-74700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as \n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{3}.\n$$\n\n \nHence, $0\\in U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider polynomials $p(x)={x}^{3},$ $q(x)=2{x}^{3},$ and $r(x)=3{x}^{3}$ from $U.$ Then: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x) & =[{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}]-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\ p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)] & ={x}^{3}-[2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-[-{x}^{3}] \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)\\ne p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-74700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-74700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-74700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-74700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $E$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{2}+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The constant polynomial $1$ belongs to $E.$\n- For the constant polynomial $1$ and any $p(x)\\in E,$ we have $1+p(x)=p(x).$\n- There are polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in E$ such that $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)\\ne p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the constant polynomial $1$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n1=0\\cdot {x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nHence, $1\\in E.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in E.$ Then, \n\n$$\n1+p(x)=1+{x}^{2}+1={x}^{2}+2\\ne p(x).\n$$\n\nThe zero-polynomial $0$ is the unique polynomial such that for any $p(x)\\in E$ we have $0+p(x)=p(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in E,$ we have \n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)],\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-94686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The constant function $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ belongs to $N.$\n- For any $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we have $f(t)-g(t)=g(t)-f(t).$\n- The addition of functions in $N$ is commutative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $N$ contains only functions that evaluate to zero at zero. However, the function defined by $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ satisfies $f(0)=1\\ne 0.$ So, $f\\notin N.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider functions $f(t)=2t$ and $g(t)=t$ in $N.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)-g(t) & =2t-t \\\\  & =t \\\\ g(t)-f(t) & =t-2t \\\\  & =-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(t)-g(t)\\ne g(t)-f(t).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=g(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of functions is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $10.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in T,$ we have $p(x)+0=0.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in T$, we have $p(x)+q(x)=p(x)-q(x).$\n- For any polynomial $p(x)\\in T$ we have $-p(x)\\in T$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}$ in $T.$ Then,\n\n$$\np(x)+0={x}^{2}+0={x}^{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=q(x)={x}^{2}\\in T.$\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x)+q(x) & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ p(x)-q(x) & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\ne p(x)-q(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x)\\in T,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x)+(-p(x))=p(x)-p(x)=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(-p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 10,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x)\\in T.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-106703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $Q$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There is a function $f(x)\\in Q,$ such that $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider the null function $f(x)=0=0{x}^{2}\\in Q.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot 0=0=f(x).\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-162664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $N$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a|x|,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in N,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider $f(x)=|x|\\in N.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot |x|=-|x|\\ne |x|.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)\\ne f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-106809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $Y$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $A\\in Y,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A=-A.$\n- For any $A\\in Y$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n- For any $A\\in Y$ we have $2A\\in Y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in Y,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot {a}_{ij}=-{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=-A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in Y,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Y,$ however $2{I}_{2}\\notin Y$ because\n\n$$\n|2{I}_{2}|=|2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0=4\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-162668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-162668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n- For any $A\\in S$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n- There is a matrix $A\\in S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A)\\ne A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For a matrix in $A\\in S,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(2A)=(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2)A=1\\cdot A=A\n$$\n\nsince the scalar multiplication of matrices is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $G$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in G,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in G$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).$\n- The scalar multiplication of functions in $G$ is associative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n(1\\cdot f)(x)=1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43"} {"question" "q-106812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "q-106812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "34f6eccacd56591bf77fc75677f49294479be73a97d2d23a6de3806b61f4d3ee"} {"question" "e-9960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times2$ real matrices whose entries are non-negative. Then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $I_2 \\in M$\n\n- For any $A \\in M$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha \\cdot A \\in M.$\n\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-9960.json" "file_sha256" "353ef04985c2ed3b0a3d725f72745305fe74becfdffccd10c47917df27672261"} {"question" "e-9960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/191451016703b3287c7e58fdb59e22effb919c97dcad7a9e3bec09efd115795a.png) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/3b13b13c14eb335adb58b3d1ff3132b4e189065033481aaf371a9376188f2443.png) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/a906a8520f0b2f676bcf97411232fa3303f87aec9dcae9abe19dca00ab6da1a7.png)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-9960.json" "file_sha256" "353ef04985c2ed3b0a3d725f72745305fe74becfdffccd10c47917df27672261"} {"question" "e-4787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L,$ we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x).$\n\n- For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$\n\n- For each $p(x) \\in L,$ we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-4787.json" "file_sha256" "6de4f28b3cb76e6eccc9ab114eb9a0eefdc8b9873f06f27449a851d1a6b69efa"} {"question" "e-4787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x), q(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x) + q(x)= q(x) +p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x) + q(x)] + r(x) = p(x) + [q(x) + r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x) \\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x) + (-p(x))= p(x) - p(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\deg(-p(x)) = \\deg(p(x)) \\leq 2,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x) \\in L.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-4787.json" "file_sha256" "6de4f28b3cb76e6eccc9ab114eb9a0eefdc8b9873f06f27449a851d1a6b69efa"} {"question" "e-8229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A= (a_{ij})$ such that $a_{11} + a_{22} > 0.$ Which of the following properties are true?\n\n- For any $A \\in P,$ we have $1 \\cdot A = A.$\n\n- For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.$\n\n- For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-8229.json" "file_sha256" "590a37215ed160d64c92139a129f2105e2f41750bd789ecfd65151b4416cc770"} {"question" "e-8229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot a_{ij} = a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta a_{ij}) = (\\alpha\\beta) a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, f