y one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ because all its entries are non-negative.\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M$. For example, let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M$.\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186124212 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L$, we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x)$.\n2. For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L$, we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]$.\n3. For each $p(x) \\in L$, we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0$."] [17592186124212 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x), q(x) \\in L$, we obtain that\n$p(x) + q(x)= q(x) +p(x)$\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L$, we obtain that\n$[p(x) + q(x)] + r(x) = p(x) + [q(x) + r(x)]$\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x) \\in L$, there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n$p(x) + (-p(x))= p(x) - p(x) = 0$.\nNotice that\n$\\deg(-p(x)) = \\deg(p(x)) \\leq 2$,\nhence $-p(x) \\in L$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186124318 :question/problem "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A= (a_{ij})$ such that $a_{11} + a_{22} > 0$. Which 